            <ArticleTitle>[The role of IL-18 in allergic rhinitis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>257-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To investigate the roles of IL-18 in the perennial allergic rhinitis (PAR).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHOD" NlmCategory="METHODS">We measured IL-18 concentration in sera of 30 PAR patients and 20 health control subjects with enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The concentration of IL-18 in PAR patients [(424.12 +/- 60. 47) ng/L] was significantly higher compared to health control subjects [(210.71 +/- 57. 37) ng/L](P&lt;0.01) and the concentration of L-18 in PAR patients with asthma [(591.19 +/- 46.54) ng/L] was significantly higher than those without asthma [(352.52 +/- 64.25) ng/L](P&lt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The IL-18 is high in PAR patients and the persistence of elevated IL-18 concentration suggests its role in the expression of persistence and exacerbation of allergic inflammation.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
